/**
  @page guides-meshes-creation Mesh creation
  @brief Construct a grid, build a tagged mesh or load an existing mesh.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  A mesh must specify its embedding coordinates and cell connectivity. Region
  and boundary attributes attach integer labels to selected entities. This
  page introduces the three construction paths used in the examples.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-uniform Generate a uniform grid

  `UniformGrid` takes a cell type and a vertex count for each coordinate axis.
  Its initial spacing is one. A triangular grid with 16 vertices per axis has
  256 vertices, 450 triangles and domain @f$[0,15]^2@f$. Scaling by @f$1/15@f$
  produces the unit square.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
  mesh.scale(1.0 / 15.0);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-uniform-2d Two-dimensional grids

  Triangular and quadrilateral grids share this coordinate convention.
  Each rectangular grid block gives two triangles or one quadrilateral.

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-uniform-3d Three-dimensional grids

  The shape argument has three entries for tetrahedra, pyramids, hexahedra
  and wedges. See @ref examples-geometry-uniformgrid for supported types and
  the resulting counts.

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-uniform-domain Domain scaling

  An axis with @f$n_i@f$ vertices spans @f$[0,n_i-1]@f$. Uniform scaling
  changes all axes by the same factor. For anisotropic domains, supply
  coordinates through the builder or apply an appropriate coordinate map.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-files Load a mesh

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-files-formats File format

  MFEM and MEDIT both use the `.mesh` extension. Pass the format explicitly.
  @ref examples-io-medit "The conversion example" writes its own input file
  and checks the loaded mesh counts.

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-files-loading Loading

  @snippet IO/MEDIT.cpp medit-convert

  @section guides-meshes-creation-builder Build vertices and cells

  The builder records vertex coordinates first, then cells using zero-based
  vertex indices. `initialize(2)` selects a two-dimensional embedding space.
  The following rectangle combines two triangles with one quadrilateral.
  Attributes one and two label its left and right regions.

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-builder-simple Connectivity

  @snippet Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp mesh-basics-build

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-builder-attributes Region attributes

  `.attribute({dimension, index}, value)` labels an entity already inserted
  into the builder. Cell index and vertex index belong to different dimensions.
  The constructed rectangle has area two; each tagged region has area one.

  @subsection guides-meshes-creation-builder-mixed Mixed cell types

  Mixed meshes share vertex indices across adjacent cells. Their local vertex
  order must follow the reference-element convention. The
  @ref guides-polytopes "polytope guide" describes the supported shapes.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-box Construct a box boundary

  `Box` takes a face type and an interval count for each embedding-space axis.
  With triangular faces, three interval counts describe a two-dimensional surface
  embedded in three dimensions. The following surface encloses the unit cube
  after scaling by one third and has area six. In this factory, three intervals
  span coordinates zero to three before scaling.

  @snippet Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp mesh-basics-box

  @section guides-meshes-creation-empty Empty meshes

  Default construction creates an empty mesh. `load()` replaces it with file
  data; a builder's `finalize()` produces a constructed mesh.
  `mesh.isEmpty()` can be checked before operations requiring cells.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-copy Mesh and field lifetimes

  Meshes can be copied and moved. Finite element spaces and grid functions
  borrow their mesh; retain that mesh and its topology for their lifetime.
  Rebuild dependent spaces and problems after changing topology. See
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts for the corresponding algebraic lifetimes.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-example Checked program

  The `MeshBasics` target validates counts, region measures, boundary extraction
  and the box surface. Its source is @ref Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp.

  @section guides-meshes-creation-see-also Continue

  - @ref guides-meshes-queries "Dimensions, counts and measures"
  - @ref guides-meshes-iteration "Traverse the constructed mesh"
  - @ref guides-meshes-io "Mesh storage"
*/
